REFERENCE (Python):
if a >= 40:
    if b >= 40: print("No")
    else:       print("Yes")
else:
    if b >= 40: print("Yes")
    else:       print("No")

IN JAVASCRIPT: same shape with braces. If the marks come from prompt() they
arrive as STRINGS, so convert with Number() first — "9" >= 40 is false but
"100" >= 40 compares character by character and misleads. Also prefer === over
== throughout.

TESTING: exclusive OR. "One of them" is not "either of them".

MARK WRONG IF:
- 45 80 prints "Yes". They wrote `a >= 40 or b >= 40`. THIS IS THE TRAP and it is
  the most common answer in the room. Test 45 80 first.
- 20 10 prints "Yes" (they inverted something).
- 40 39 prints "No" — the boundary; 40 passes.

CHECK: 45 80, then 45 30.
A correct one-liner using != on two comparisons is fine and rather good.

REFERENCE (Python):
if a < b:
    if b < c: print("Increasing")
    else:     print("Not sorted")
else:
    if a > b:
        if b > c: print("Decreasing")
        else:     print("Not sorted")
    else:
        print("Not sorted")

IN JAVASCRIPT: same shape with braces. WATCH THE STRINGS — this is where it
bites hardest. "10" < "9" is TRUE, because strings compare character by
character. A student whose 1 2 3 works but whose 9 10 11 says "Not sorted" has
forgotten Number(). Test them on 9 10 11.

TESTING: three outcomes from two comparisons, and that equal values belong in
neither category.

MARK WRONG IF:
- 2 2 3 prints "Increasing". They used <= instead of <. THE TRAP.
- 5 5 5 prints anything other than "Not sorted".
- 1 3 2 prints "Increasing" — they only compared the first pair.
- No "Not sorted" branch at all, so it prints nothing for mixed input.

CHECK: 2 2 3, then 5 5 5, then 1 3 2.

REFERENCE (Python):
if x == 0:
    if y == 0: print("Origin")
    else:      print("On Y axis")
else:
    if y == 0: print("On X axis")
    else:
        if x > 0:
            if y > 0: print("Quadrant 1")
            else:     print("Quadrant 4")
        else:
            if y > 0: print("Quadrant 2")
            else:     print("Quadrant 3")

IN JAVASCRIPT: same shape with braces, but use === for the zero checks.
x == 0 is true for the string "0" AND for an empty string, so == will appear to
work and then fail on odd input. Convert with Number() and compare with ===.

TESTING: handling the special cases BEFORE the general ones. Zero is neither
positive nor negative, so it has to be dealt with first or it falls through into
a quadrant.

MARK WRONG IF:
- 0 5 prints "Quadrant 2" (or any quadrant). They tested x > 0 / else, so zero
  went down the negative branch. THE TRAP — test 0 5 and 5 0 first.
- 0 0 prints "On X axis" or "On Y axis" instead of "Origin".
- Only the four quadrants are handled and the axis cases print nothing.

CHECK: 0 5, 5 0, 0 0.
A student who wrote the three zero checks first, before touching quadrants, has
understood the question. Say so.
